To create a captivating and engaging introductory speech using C++ code, you can utilize a combination of appropriate text and ASCII art to visually draw the audience in, while also incorporating dynamic elements that introduce the topic and tease the audience's curiosity. Here's an example for a speech on the "Impact of Artificial Intelligence":

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <ctime>

int main() {
    std::cout << "********************************************\n"
                 "* Welcome to the Impact of Artificial Intelligence *\n"
                 "********************************************\n\n";

    std::vector<std::string> hooks = {
        "Did you know that experts predict AI could contribute up to $15.7 trillion to the global economy by 2030?\n",
        "Did you know that by 2025, the global AI market is projected to reach $60 billion?\n",
        "An interesting fact: AI can now identify objects in images with more accuracy than humans can!\n"
    };

    std::srand(std::time(0));
    int randomIndex = std::rand() % hooks.size();
    std::cout << hooks[randomIndex];

    std::cout << "\nIn today's presentation, I'll be taking you on an insightful journey exploring the world of Artificial "
                 "Intelligence. Here's a sneak peek at what we'll learn together:\n"
                 "\n1. The History of Artificial Intelligence\n"
                 "2. How AI is Transforming Industries\n"
                 "3. Ethical Considerations and AI's Future\n"
                 "\nNow, without further ado, let's dive into the fascinating world of Artificial Intelligence!\n";

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the code prints an eye-catching opening statement with ASCII art. It uses a vector, `hooks`, to store several hooks, anecdotes, or compelling statistics and selects a random item from the list by using the `rand()` function and the system time.

After the hook, the code introduces the topic and provides an overview of what the presentation will cover, listing the key points in an organized manner. This generates a clear and informative preview of the content for the audience while still retaining an element of curiosity.

Compile and run the above code; this output will serve as your opening statement in your presentation. Since it picks the hook randomly every time, you'll have a unique start each time you run the code!

