When looking at the given code (in particular, the highlighted piece), I believe there are multiple issues preventing the desired functionality. These include:

1. The code assumes that there is only ever one element with the class "rating-stars" - this is not the case, which leads to issues when attempting to dynamically add the element. Instead, using the `document.getElementsByClassName` method is preferred, as it will return a NodeList of the matching elements, allowing for looping through all the elements.

2. The code makes repeated use of the `document.querySelector` method, which returns the first matching element it can find. In this case, the first occurrence of "rating-stars" is the newly created element, which means that the element with the actual rating buttons is not being accessed. Instead, an ID should be used to select the element with the rating buttons.

3. The code accesses the rating buttons using the class selector ".rating-stars button", which will only match the buttons inside the newly created element, and not the built-in rating buttons. Instead, the CSS selector ".rating-buttons button" can be used to select all buttons with the class "rating-button".

4. The code attempts to update the titles and classes of the buttons based on the rating provided, however this is based on the assumption that there is a 1:1 mapping between the rating buttons and the stars. This is not the case, and there are more stars than buttons. It's also unclear how the code handles the case where there is no current rating. A better approach would be to use the number of filled stars as the rating, which can be done using the "querySelectorAll" method to select all the stars with the class "filled-star", and then counting the number of filled stars.

5. The updateElements function updates the current rating, but does not update the actual rating buttons. Instead, this should be done in the updateTitles and updateClasses functions, which can then also be called when the rating is updated.

6. The code assumes that there is only ever one instance of the video player, which is not the case. Instead, a more robust approach would be to loop through all the video players and update the ratings for each one.

